---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AtmosphereNav from '../../../components/AtmosphereNav.astro';
---

<Layout>
	<Breadcrumb crumbs={[
		{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
		{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' },
		{ label: 'U.S. Standard Atmosphere Calculator' },
	]} />
	<h1>U.S. Standard Atmosphere Calculator</h1>
	<AtmosphereNav active="notes" />
	<h2>About the U.S. Standard Atmosphere</h2>
	<p>
	The <a href="https://www.pdas.com/atmosdownload.html" target="_blank">U.S. Standard Atmosphere</a> (USSA) models atmospheric properties by 
	altitude from -5 to 1000 km (-16404 to 3280840 ft, -3.11 to 621.37 mi), representing typical mid-latitude conditions. Formulas are 
	from the file ussa1976.dpr in <a href="https://www.pdas.com/atmosdownload.html" target="_blank">PDAS</a>. This calculator uses the 
	values of the Avogadro and gas constants fixed in the 2019 SI revision, rather than those used in the 1976 USSA, as well as updated gas 
	concentrations from NOAA's Global Monitoring Laboratory to better represent current atmospheric chemistry, particularly the increase in 
	CO<sub>2</sub>. This results in a slightly higher molar mass (28.9657 g/mol rather than 28.9644) and slightly different values for density 
	and the speed of sound. Properties are calculated for dry air; humid air can be up to 2% less dense than dry air at the same temperature and 
	pressure.
	</p>
	<p>
	The boiling point of water is calculated 
	using <a href="https://www.iapws.org/relguide/IF97-Rev.html" target="_blank">IAPWS-95</a> and is accurate to 0.01°C (0.02°F). At pressures below 
	water's triple point of 611.657 Pa, the boiling point is undefined because liquid water cannot exist.
	</p>
	<p>
	The U.S. Standard Atmosphere models a typical mid-latitude profile. At a given altitude, it generally underestimates pressure in the tropics and 
	in summer, and overestimates it in polar regions and in winter. It is not a weather model, and it does not account for local variations in pressure, 
	temperature, or humidity (it assumes 0% humidity throughout). Water vapor and ozone are not included because their concentrations vary drastically 
	based on weather conditions, latitude, and season. Above the stratopause (50 km), predicted conditions at a given sea-level temperature and pressure
	may vary widely from actual conditions.
	</p>
	<h2>Standard sea-level conditions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Pressure: 1 atm (101325 Pa, 1013.25 hPa, 760 mmHg, 14.6959 psi, 29.9213 inHg)</li>
		<li>Temperature: 15°C (59°F, 288.15 K, 518.67°R)</li>
		<li>Lapse rate: -6.5°C/km (0.00357°F/ft) under 11 km, varies above 11 km</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Changes with altitude</h2>
	<p>
	In the troposphere (below 11 km), temperature decreases with altitude at the lapse rate. Above that, the stratosphere warms with altitude due to the
	presence of UV-absorbing ozone; the mesosphere (50-86 km) cools again; and the thermosphere (86-1000 km) warms again. Pressure decreases roughly
	exponentially throughout the atmosphere.
	</p>
	<p>
	Pressure is adjusted to sea-level two ways: <b>sea-level pressure</b> and <b>altimeter setting</b>. Sea-level pressure computes the air pressure
	at sea-level given the measured pressure and temperature, standard lapse rate, the altitude of the measurement, and the ideal gas law. Altimeter
	setting does the same, but assumes a constant sea-level temperature of 15°C (59°F) with the same lapse rate.
	</p>
	<p>
	Example: at Mount Everest (8849 m, 29032 ft), typical summer conditions 
	are <a href="https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wea.3931" target="_blank">-19°C (-2°F)</a> and <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10066724/" target="_blank">336 hPa (0.332 atm)</a>. 
	Extrapolating -19°C to sea level with the standard lapse rate gives 37.4°C (99.3°F) - far above the standard 15°C - and a sea-level pressure of 980 
	hPa. Altimeter setting instead assumes the standard 15°C at sea level and asks what pressure would produce that same 336 hPa at 8849 m: 1081 hPa.
	</p>
	<h2>Geometric versus geopotential altitude</h2>
	<p>
	Geometric altitude is the actual height above mean sea level, while geopotential altitude is a "corrected" altitude representing the altitude in a
	uniform gravitational field of 9.80665 m/s<sup>2</sup> that would require the same work to reach as the actual altitude in the real gravity field. 
	Gravity weakens slightly at higher altitudes, due to the greater distance from earth's center, so at very high altitudes geopotential altitude is 
	significantly less than geometric altitude.
	</p>
</Layout>
